/**
  @page examples-pdes-stokes Solving the Stokes equations
  @brief Couple velocity and pressure in a lid-driven cavity.

  A moving lid drags a viscous fluid around a closed square cavity. The Stokes
  model neglects inertia, leaving a linear system in velocity and pressure.
  This example introduces mixed finite elements and a pressure normalization
  before the @ref examples-pdes-navierstokes "Navier–Stokes tutorial" adds
  convection and time evolution.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-pdes-stokes-model The mathematical problem

  In @f$\Omega=(0,1)^2@f$, solve
  @f[
    -\nu\Delta u+\nabla p=0,\qquad \operatorname{div}u=0.
  @f]
  The velocity @f$u:\Omega\to\mathbb R^2@f$ and pressure
  @f$p:\Omega\to\mathbb R@f$ are coupled by momentum balance and
  incompressibility. The kinematic viscosity is @f$\nu=1@f$ in nondimensional
  units. There is no body force.

  On the lid @f$y=1@f$, prescribe @f$u=(16x^2(1-x)^2,0)@f$. The other three
  walls have @f$u=0@f$. This regularized lid reaches unit speed at its midpoint
  and vanishes at both corners, avoiding the discontinuous boundary data of a
  constant-speed lid. Reference comparisons require the same regularized boundary profile.

  Only the pressure gradient enters momentum balance. The condition
  @f$\int_\Omega p\,dx=0@f$ fixes its arbitrary additive constant.

  @section examples-pdes-stokes-weak The mixed weak formulation

  With test velocity @f$v@f$ vanishing on the boundary, test pressure @f$q@f$,
  and scalar test value @f$\eta@f$, the equations are
  @f[
    \begin{aligned}
      \nu\int_\Omega\nabla u:\nabla v\,dx-\int_\Omega p\operatorname{div}v\,dx&=0,\\
      \int_\Omega q\operatorname{div}u\,dx+\lambda\int_\Omega q\,dx&=0,\\
      \eta\int_\Omega p\,dx&=0.
    \end{aligned}
  @f]
  The global multiplier @f$\lambda@f$ enforces the pressure gauge. The integral constraint fixes the additive pressure constant.

  @section examples-pdes-stokes-mesh 1. Construct and label the cavity

  A 17-by-17 vertex grid produces 512 triangles on the unit square. Boundary
  edges on the top are labeled `lid`; the remaining exterior edges are `walls`.

  @snippet PDEs/Stokes.cpp stokes-mesh

  @section examples-pdes-stokes-form 2. Choose compatible spaces

  Quadratic continuous velocity and linear continuous pressure form the
  Taylor–Hood pair. Using equal-order spaces without a suitable stabilization
  would be a different discretization with a different stability question.
  `P0g` supplies the single global pressure-mean multiplier.

  @snippet PDEs/Stokes.cpp stokes-form

  The Jacobian inner product is the componentwise velocity-gradient term.
  The pressure term couples to the divergence of the test velocity, while the
  continuity equation couples the trial velocity to the pressure test function.
  The lid profile is interpolated into the quadratic velocity trace space.

  @section examples-pdes-stokes-output 3. Solve and inspect the circulation

  The mixed formulation gives an indefinite saddle-point system. Sparse LU is adequate
  for this small demonstration; larger mixed problems call for suitable block
  preconditioning. Solver success is checked and the algebraic residual is reported before
  inspecting the exported fields.

  @snippet PDEs/Stokes.cpp stokes-solve

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages}

  @parblock
  @image html PDEs/Stokes-speed.png "Computed speed in the regularized Stokes cavity."
  @image html PDEs/Stokes-streamlines.png "Instantaneous streamlines and velocity directions."
  @endparblock

  Open `Stokes.xdmf`, color by the magnitude of **velocity**, and use ParaView's
  **Stream Tracer** to inspect the circulation. Pressure is exported separately
  as **pressure**. ParaView integrates the displayed streamlines through the exported velocity field.

  Incompressibility is imposed in the pressure test space. Algebraic residuals and
  mesh refinement assess the numerical solution.

  @section examples-pdes-stokes-full Complete program

  See @ref PDEs/Stokes.cpp "Stokes.cpp", the
  @ref examples-pdes-navierstokes "transient cavity example", and the
  @ref guides-solvers "solver guide".

  @example PDEs/Stokes.cpp @m_examplenavigation{examples-pdes-stokes,PDEs/} @m_footernavigation
*/
